---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const ciaCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Confidentiality', note: 'Only the right people can read it. Enforced by encryption and access control; broken by leaks, interception and shoulder-surfing.' },
    { math: '2 · Integrity', note: 'Data says what it is supposed to say. Enforced by hashes, signatures and audit trails; broken by tampering, corruption and sneaky edits.' },
    { math: '3 · Availability', note: 'The system works when needed. Enforced by redundancy and capacity; broken by outages, ransomware and — classically — denial-of-service floods.' },
    { math: '4 · The trade-offs are real', note: 'Maximum confidentiality means air-gaps nobody can use; maximum availability means no locks. Security is choosing where to sit between them.' },
    { math: '5 · Everything maps to risk', note: 'A threat (who/what could harm you) × a vulnerability (your weakness) = a risk. Security spends money where risk × impact is largest.' },
  ],
};

const layersCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'phish', label: 'phishing success rate', min: 1, max: 30, step: 1, value: 10, unit: '%' },
    { name: 'ml', label: 'malware evasion rate', min: 1, max: 60, step: 1, value: 25, unit: '%' },
    { name: 'creds', label: 'stolen-credential reuse rate', min: 1, max: 40, step: 1, value: 15, unit: '%' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'attackers passing ALL layers', expr: 'phish*ml*creds/10000', unit: '%' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/cybersecurity/foundational/security-foundations-the-cia-triad">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Cybersecurity is the discipline of keeping systems trustworthy against intelligent opposition.
    Its shared vocabulary is the <strong>CIA triad</strong> — confidentiality, integrity,
    availability — and its economics is risk: you cannot defend everything, so you measure what an
    attack would cost and defend accordingly. Every framework you will meet is an elaboration of
    those two ideas.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the triad and its trade-offs" widget="equationStep" config={ciaCfg} />

  <h2>Defence in depth, quantified</h2>
  <p>
    No single control is reliable — each blocks some fraction of attacks. But controls stack
    multiplicatively: an attacker must clear every layer, so a 10% phishing success, 25% malware
    evasion and 15% credential-reuse rate leave roughly one attacker in 270 through all three.
    Depth is the defender's compounding interest.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="layer three controls and multiply the survivors" widget="sliderLab" config={layersCfg} />

  <h2>Know your adversary's economics</h2>
  <p>
    Attackers have budgets too. Bulk phishing is cheap and sloppy; targeted intrusions are
    expensive and patient. The defender's strategy follows: make cheap attacks fail cheaply (raise
    their cost), and assume the expensive ones will occasionally succeed — which is why detection
    and response matter as much as prevention.
  </p>

  <h2>The framework landscape</h2>
  <p>
    The field organises itself through shared frameworks: NIST's NICE Workforce Framework names the
    jobs (defend, analyse, govern, build), NIST's Cybersecurity Framework names the functions
    (identify, protect, detect, respond, recover), and ISO 27001 certifies management systems. They
    all trade in the same currency: identified risks, chosen controls, demonstrated improvement.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NIST SP 800-181r1 (NICE Framework); NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0; Bishop, <em>Computer Security: Art and Science</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
